{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1370367-can-we-talk-about-nightmare","id":1370367,"title":"Can we talk about Nightmare?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-02-25T08:50:05-08:00","id":1370367,"title":"Can we talk about Nightmare?","content":"So one of my players has been using the Nightmare Discipline pretty heavily and it seems really strong for a player who is a bit creative. This applies especially to Nightmare 4. Since it effects all the senses, we have had situations where the player has tried to incapacitate other Vampires by creating the illusion of chains/vines/snakes that wrap around them, and I find it very hard to argue against this given the way the discipline is written, but this also seems much too powerful, especially for a 4 dot effect.<br /> <br /> It seems very hard to build a character who can resist the effects of Nightmare. This is also true for Dominate and Majesty, but those powers are a little more subtle and less useful in combat. With Nightmare 5, you can easily do lethal damage to other Vampires, and unless the target is an elder with exceptional Composure, it's very unlikely that their Composure + Blood Potency will beat the attacker who is built for Nightmare. What's more, unlike Dominate or Majesty, most Nightmare powers do not require Dread Presence to set up (although Nightmare 5 does need to be set up with either Nightmare 2 or Nightmare 3).<br /> <br /> I'm also curious how other splats interact with Dread Presence. It seems the only way to resist it is via Predatory Aura, which only Vampires have. At the same time, denying willpower usage for free with no way to counteract it seems a little too powerful for a 1-dot effect.<br /> <br /> What do other people think? How have you reigned in the nightmare discipline in your games?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2384-aurumae","name":"Aurumae","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2384_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"191"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-25T11:10:48-08:00","id":1370405,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aurumae</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1370367-can-we-talk-about-nightmare#post1370367\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So one of my players has been using the Nightmare Discipline pretty heavily and it seems really strong for a player who is a bit creative. This applies especially to Nightmare 4. Since it effects all the senses, we have had situations where the player has tried to incapacitate other Vampires by creating the illusion of chains/vines/snakes that wrap around them, and I find it very hard to argue against this given the way the discipline is written, but this also seems much too powerful, especially for a 4 dot effect.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's just an illusion, it isn't real. If snakes or chains wrap around you, how would you respond to that? How many vampires would just accept being tied down and not struggle at all? Most vampires would probably try to break out of the binding chains by force, maybe snap the chains apart with Vigor and/or Physical Intensity, etc. The moment you try to break the chains, you discover there aren't any real chains holding you back, and the illusion falls apart.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":25286,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25286-tomorrow-s-nobody","user":"Tomorrow's Nobody","date":". 02-25-2020, 03:15 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25286-tomorrow-s-nobody","name":"Tomorrow's Nobody","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25286_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"75"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-25T12:08:54-08:00","id":1370419,"title":"","content":"On the subject of Nightmare 4, I think there's a subtle, but distinct, difference between an illusion and a hallucination. Especially given the examples Nightmare 4 offers. None of them boast the interactivity that &quot;wrap them in snakes&quot; does. The closest it comes is creating &quot;a shambling monster that stalks her victims&quot; which reads to me like it makes something that hangs back and <i>acts</i> scary, not something that <i>actually</i> physically confront the vampire's victims.<br /> <br /> Obviously, it can be interpreted differently, especially with the clause explaining that it can't actually hurt victims, but I think that's more an insurance policy to keep it from being &quot;too good&quot; and/or overlapping too much with Nightmare 5. Like, if you engulf a room in leering hellfire and the people inside decide to dash through it, they won't actually take damage mechanically, even if they feel like they got burned or whatever your hellfire does.<br /> <br /> And if you don't agree with my interpretation, that's okay! Maybe try talking to your player about it? Obviously it's impacting your fun and cheapening the drama of the game, so bring that up, and maybe you can work out an acceptable solution together?<br /> <br /> On Nightmare 5, it costs a point of Willpower to activate and has no weapon modifier. Sure, it has the <i>potential</i> to hit hard, but you can whiff it like any other pool, and each time you try you burn a resource that's harder to come by than blood. Plus, there's no special clause saying damage from Nightmare is exempt from being soaked by Resilience. And if you still think it's too good, you can rule that it deals bashing to Kindred.<br /> <br /> Dread Presence is just good, although keep in mind it doesn't stop spending Willpower on resistance [or to activate cool powers], like, you know, bolstering Composure to avoid getting ganked by Nightmare 5.<br /> <br /> Anyway, I hope my rambling helps!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Posting gives me anxiety, but I'm still happy to be here</i><br /> Habitual Lurker, Vengrel Liker, Acolyte Apologist, She/Her","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25955-somethingfishy","name":"SomethingFishy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25955_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"94"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-25T22:36:53-08:00","id":1370508,"title":"","content":"I might be misremembering, but I think Dread Presence prevents the victim from spending willpower to directly oppose you -- but they can spend it in self-defense. So, if I'm suddenly chained up during combat, I know I'm burning willpower to wriggle out.<br /> <br /> Once I resist the chains, my arms are going to move through where they should be. The Delusional Condition resolves if someone disproves the delusion, so it would make sense for a character to at least be able to question their environment when hallucinating. And if not, maybe I feel like my arms are still tied down, when in fact I'm flailing with all my strength (and Vigor, and Physical Intensity). So I might be flinging around.<br /> <br /> Also, the book says that the hallucination is interactive. So <i>do</i> interact with it. The victim struggles to get out. Are they able to? If they do, how do they react to the rest of their environment? How does the hallucination persist? Maybe wherever the chains touched, they bit in, and now the victim feels a burning sensation as black veins grow from the minor &quot;wounds&quot; toward their heart. <br /> <br /> The Nightmare 4 description mentions that the hallucinations follow a nightmare logic. So make your PC engage with that. How does the NPC respond, and how does that create unintended consequence? One of my PCs is basically a god-tier manipulator, and working to give a &quot;Yes <i>and...</i>&quot; response has made things a lot more fun.<br /> <br /> This is all the kind of stuff that I'm bad at, in the moment. But I think if you put together maybe five to ten rough plans, you'll be in a position to do some interesting stuff. <br /> <br /> PS. Also, I want to second what SomethingFishy said. If it's causing a lot of frustration, it may be worth having a conversation with your player.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24634-dubiousruffian","name":"DubiousRuffian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"210"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}